\section{Computed on request, not at fit time}{

\code{\link[=fit_influence]{fit_influence()}} returns estimates, and this reads a completed fit. A fitted
object carries what was estimated, and quantities derived to answer a further
question are computed when that question is asked. An effect size is a derived
magnitude: it picks a range, evaluates a change over it, and reports that as the
practical size of the effect.

Its Selection counterpart is \code{\link[=selection_effect]{selection_effect()}}.

A mechanism with several columns (such as \code{\link[=bi_components]{bi_components()}}) has no single
column to give a contrast, so its term has no entry here.
}

\section{What the interval covers}{

A Wald interval built from the fit's own \code{coef()} and \code{vcov()}, with the
same critical value as \link[=netis-influence-methods]{confint()}: a \code{t} quantile
on the residual degrees of freedom for an ordinary least squares fit, a \code{t}
quantile on the coefficient's Satterthwaite degrees of freedom for an \code{lme4}
fit, and a normal quantile for an \code{sna::lnam} fit. It covers uncertainty in the
COEFFICIENTS. The percentile range the change is evaluated over is taken
from the estimating sample and treated as fixed, so the interval does not
carry uncertainty about the range itself.
}

\section{On a simultaneous (\code{sna::lnam}) fit}{

There the entry holds \code{coefficient_times_contrast}, in place of
\code{predicted_change}: the coefficient times the contrast range, a \strong{coefficient-scale
quantity that excludes feedback}. It is not the change in the outcome from changing
the predictor, for any actor or on average. In \verb{y = rho * W1 y + X b + e} a
predictor reaches the outcome through \code{(I - rho * W1)^-1}, so a change in one actor's
predictor moves every actor connected to it in \code{W1}, and comes back. That quantity
depends on \code{rho}, on \code{W1} and on which actors change, and rnetis does not compute it.
The result says so (\code{excludes_network_feedback}, \code{scale}, \code{scope_note}), and so do
its printed form and \link[=plot.netis_influence]{plot()}. The same-outcome term itself has
no entry: its coefficient is \code{rho}, not a slope over a contrast.
}

\section{The range is a convention, and it is named}{

The contrast runs between two values of the predictor, and rnetis picks them
by default: the observed 10th and 90th percentiles in the estimating
sample. That is a \strong{reporting convention}, this package's own, and not part of
any effect-size definition, so it is settable through \code{probs} and reported back
on the result: \code{contrast_low} and \code{contrast_high} on each entry, \code{contrast_probs}
and \code{contrast_rule} on an unmoderated entry, and \code{contrast_probs}, \code{scale} and
\code{uncertainty} on the whole.
